---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "Circuit Breaker Safety Guide: Sizing and the 80% Rule | Ampstowatt";
const description = "Learn how to safely size your circuit breakers, convert amps to watts, and why you can only use 80% of a breaker's capacity for continuous loads.";
const h1Title = "Circuit Breaker Safety Guide: Sizing and the 80% Rule";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/blog/circuit-breaker-safety-guide/"
  h1Title={h1Title}
  kicker="Safety Guide"
>
  <section class="card article-content">
    <h1>{h1Title}</h1>
    <p>When planning electrical loads for your home, understanding how circuit breakers work is critical for safety. Circuit breakers are designed to protect your home's wiring from overheating and causing fires. They do this by cutting off the power if the current (amps) exceeds safe limits.</p>

    <h2>The Relationship Between Amps, Watts, and Circuit Breakers</h2>
    <p>Most appliances are rated in <strong>watts</strong> (power consumption), but circuit breakers are rated in <strong>amps</strong> (current flow). To determine if your circuit can handle an appliance, you must convert watts to amps (or amps to watts).</p>
    <p>The basic formula for a standard 120V US household circuit is:</p>
    <div style="background: var(--bg-card); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; font-family: monospace; text-align: center; font-size: 1.2rem; border: 1px solid var(--border); margin: 1rem 0;">
      Watts = Amps × Volts
    </div>
    <p>For example, a 15-amp circuit breaker at 120V can theoretically handle up to 1,800 watts (15 × 120 = 1,800). But there's a catch: the 80% rule.</p>

    <h2>What is the 80% Continuous Load Rule?</h2>
    <p>The National Electrical Code (NEC) specifies that a circuit breaker should only be loaded to <strong>80% of its maximum capacity</strong> for continuous loads. A "continuous load" is defined as any device that runs for three hours or more consecutively (e.g., space heaters, EV chargers, or air conditioners).</p>
    
    <h3>Why the 80% Limit?</h3>
    <p>Circuit breakers use thermal mechanics to trip. When current flows, it generates heat. If you run a breaker at 100% capacity continuously, the heat builds up in the panel and can cause the breaker to trip prematurely, or worse, degrade the wire insulation over time.</p>

    <h2>Common Breaker Sizes and Their True Limits</h2>
    <table style="width: 100%; text-align: left; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 2rem 0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);">
          <th>Breaker Size</th>
          <th>Voltage</th>
          <th>Max Theoretical Watts</th>
          <th>Safe Continuous Watts (80%)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><strong>15 Amp</strong></td>
          <td>120V</td>
          <td>1,800 W</td>
          <td><strong style="color: var(--brand-orange);">1,440 W</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><strong>20 Amp</strong></td>
          <td>120V</td>
          <td>2,400 W</td>
          <td><strong style="color: var(--brand-orange);">1,920 W</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><strong>30 Amp</strong></td>
          <td>240V</td>
          <td>7,200 W</td>
          <td><strong style="color: var(--brand-orange);">5,760 W</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding: 0.5rem 0;"><strong>50 Amp</strong></td>
          <td>240V</td>
          <td>12,000 W</td>
          <td><strong style="color: var(--brand-orange);">9,600 W</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Real-World Example: The Space Heater Problem</h2>
    <p>A standard space heater draws 1,500 watts. If you plug it into a 15-amp circuit (which has a continuous safe limit of 1,440 watts), and leave it running overnight, you are exceeding the safe continuous threshold. While it might not trip the breaker immediately (since it's below the 1,800W theoretical maximum), it creates a dangerous heat buildup over time.</p>

    <div style="background: rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.1); border-left: 4px solid var(--brand-orange); padding: 1rem; margin: 2rem 0;">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Use our <a href="/120v-amps-to-watts/">120V Amps to Watts Calculator</a> to quickly check the loads on your household circuits before plugging in multiple high-draw appliances like microwaves, toasters, or hair dryers on the same breaker.
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
